= Toronto network exploration

== Site layers and inspection

The Toronto network panel places site search near the top and displays TELUS and Bell outdoor records as separate, independently controlled layers. TELUS indoor locations remain visible. All enabled licensed outdoor locations remain selectable independently of fan eligibility. The current TELUS extract is classified as indoor, so those records are represented as locations rather than verified 3D towers.

The network view-model maintains the selected site, provider filters, search and planning inputs separately from the map and panel. Closing the network panel keeps the enabled sites, fans and selected bearing on the map; opening *Network* or selecting a site reopens the panel. Layer switches still control what is displayed. Data failures remain visible and can be retried by toggling the affected layer.

The user can search licensed sites by reported location, identifier, coordinates or licensee; the list shows up to eight nearest matches. Selecting a location displays licensee, record count, technologies, site type, antenna information and available IDs. The user can expand reported records, select a bearing, focus fans on a selected site and adjust fan intensity.

At zoom 14 and above, arrows indicate reported directional bearings. Qualifying purpose-built outdoor towers can be drawn as schematic 3D structures when all records at a coordinate agree on a valid reported structure height. The record data and model are not independent confirmation of the actual structure or current service.

== Radio planning fans

Bell locations with exactly three reported directional bearings and usable radio inputs can show three color-coded planning fans. A selected Bell site can also show usable bearings when it has a different bearing count. Colors represent bearing order, not a unique cell identity.

The estimate uses a 3GPP urban macro mean path-loss model with user-adjustable LOS or NLOS mode, assumed EIRP and received-power threshold. Defaults are NLOS, 46 dBm and -100 dBm. The overview fan is drawn at 5/12 of its calculated radius and uses a minimum displayed beamwidth of 15 degrees. The panel distinguishes calculated and displayed radius.

#block(fill: rgb("edf4ee"), inset: 10pt, radius: 3pt)[
  *Interpretation.* Fans are illustrative link-budget estimates. They are not measured coverage, RSRP or SINR results, and do not model buildings, terrain obstruction, interference, loading, fading or penetration loss. A Bell license does not prove TELUS service at an individual location.
]

= Project area features

== Default projects

When Toronto loads, the map shows a dashed boundary for each of its four projects. With no saved edit, the viewer chooses the eligible Bell location nearest the project center as an anchor, then the six eligible locations nearest that anchor. Eligibility requires three usable sector estimates under the default assumptions. There is no hard distance limit; the Project Overview reports the maximum distance from the first site so that a sparse cluster can be recognized.

For each project, the outline encloses the full-radius sector geometries of its current sites. It is a single convex hull, which may include gaps between fans. It is a project area, not a prediction that every point within it has service. Selecting a project fits its boundary in a top-down map view and opens Tasks in the right-side popup. The separate full-page Project Overview is opened from the sidebar, task panel or active-project shortcut. It contains the project name, site count, cluster radius, planning notes, site list and saved task summaries. Back to map returns to geographic inspection; Map task editor opens the right-side editor, and task management remains available in the lower Overview table. The popup has Tasks, Virtual UEs and Network switches and one close button. Closing or switching views preserves the selected sites and map boundaries. On a phone, navigation closes and the panel scrolls within a limited height; map fitting keeps the boundary clear of the panel and camera controls.

== Virtual UE editor

Select a project and choose *Virtual UEs* in the map-side panel, or choose *Virtual UE editor* in the Virtual UEs section below Drive-test tasks in Project Overview. Create a named planning UE, enter WGS84 latitude and longitude or choose *Place on map* and click a location, then save. Saved UEs can be selected, edited or deleted with confirmation. SQLite keeps individual UEs separate by region and project; failed saves preserve the draft for retry. Drafts remain in the current browser session across project switches and are lost if the page is left without saving. The editor stores planning locations only: it does not calculate radio performance, attach UEs to routes or establish actual device locations.

== Project use cases

After selecting a project, choose a category from USE CASES in the left navigation or Use case in Project Overview: Virtual Drive Test, Parameter A/B Test or AI-RAN Algo. Benchmark. The selection saves immediately to SQLite and both dropdowns stay synchronized. Choose Uncategorized to clear it. Existing projects start uncategorized, and original project names, site memberships, task routes and speed cases are retained.

Categories apply independently to every project in Toronto and New York. They describe the project's purpose; selecting one does not execute a drive test, parameter comparison or algorithm benchmark. Controls are disabled while loading or saving. A failed save restores the last accepted value and allows the selection to be retried; unavailable category data has a Retry loading use cases action.

== Editing project sites

After selecting a Toronto project, right-click a visible Bell site and use the popup to add it to, or remove it from, that project. A site added through the UI must have three usable default-scenario sector estimates. The server saves the site IDs in SQLite; after the save succeeds, the site list, project boundary and fitted map view are updated. A failed save leaves the previous membership in place and provides a retry action.

The original project target is seven sites. Edits may produce a different count; the planning UI reports that target. A nonempty valid set can have an enclosing boundary. The integrated virtual-drive model uses available eligible planning transmitters and does not require exactly seven. If a saved site disappears from the current source dataset, the affected project boundary and editing are unavailable rather than silently removing the missing site.

Project edits are saved before the displayed membership changes. If a save fails, the previous membership remains visible and the action offers a retry. Selection, membership and save state are separated from the map and controls in the project view-model; this does not change the radio-planning limitations.

New York project shortcuts navigate to their configured locations, but project boundaries and editable radio site membership are unavailable because the reservation records do not contain the needed radio inputs.

= New York reservations

The New York view uses the NYC pole reservation snapshot filtered to franchisee NY SMSA, associated with Verizon. The downloaded snapshot described in the repository contains 701 records: 125 installed, 386 approved and 190 proposed. Installed and approved reservations appear as map markers. Proposed reservations remain searchable in the panel but are not drawn as markers. A selected reservation shows available street, borough, pole, status and date fields.

Reservation loading, search and selection state are maintained by a view-model separate from the map and panel. Closing the panel leaves enabled markers on the map; opening *Verizon reservations* or selecting a marker reopens it. Turning the layer off and back on retries a failed data load.

These are administrative reservations, not verified operating cell sites. They do not establish live service and do not supply antenna bearings, widths or frequencies. Consequently, the viewer does not generate New York sector fans or project coverage boundaries.

= Project drive-test tasks

Every Toronto and New York project supports a separate list of tasks. New task creates a draft with a suggested ID; users can define the ID and task name, then Save task. IDs are unique within the project and use 1-64 letters, digits, hyphens or underscores. Names contain 1-120 characters. Saved tasks can be reopened, renamed or deleted. Each task may have no routes initially, or multiple named routes.

*Map Settings > Road fitting* is a checkbox, enabled by default. Turning it off keeps new drawn and imported routes on their input paths, disables Fit to roads and cancels pending fitting. Completed fits remain unchanged. Turning it on affects subsequent fitting actions; it does not refit existing routes automatically. The setting applies across projects in the current page and resets to on after reload or a region change.

Draw on map previews successive waypoints as dashed segments. With Road fitting enabled, Finish route automatically requests a driving path through the waypoints in their chosen order, moving the route endpoints onto the road network where possible. With it disabled, Finish retains the waypoint path. Undo point removes the last waypoint; Cancel drawing or Escape discards the unfinished drawing. The route service chooses a driving route between guide points, so users can add more waypoints to guide the intended road choice.

Import GeoJSON accepts LineString and MultiLineString geometry, Features and FeatureCollections containing those geometries. Feature names are retained where supplied and altitude is discarded. Invalid GeoJSON is rejected as a whole without replacing existing routes. Valid imported paths are added to the draft and, when Road fitting is enabled, fitted sequentially. Paths with more than 100 points use up to 100 guide points selected by preserving endpoints and the largest bends; this can change details of the original trace. Fit to roads also works on existing routes while Road fitting is enabled. Successful fits retain guide points for later refitting.

Road-fitted paths are solid blue; original paths that have not been fitted are dashed amber with a visible status. If the service is unavailable, cannot find a connected driving path within 150 m of the guide points, or returns geometry exceeding application limits, the original is retained for review and retry. A failed refit keeps the previous fitted geometry. Progress and Stop road fitting are provided; cancellation and project changes prevent late responses from replacing the current draft. Save task is disabled while fitting is pending. Stop keeps the paths already present in the draft, which can then be saved or discarded.

The default routing service is #link("https://routing.openstreetmap.de/about.html")[FOSSGIS OSRM], using OpenStreetMap road data. Guide coordinates are sent to that service. The UI displays attribution and service/privacy links. The server makes at most one upstream request per second, permits one in flight, and caches the last 32 successful guide-point requests for its lifetime. Operators may set `NDT_ROUTING_URL` to another OSRM-compatible base URL. Upstream requests time out after 8 seconds; the browser times out after 12 seconds. Road and basemap snapshots can differ; a fitted path is not live road-access verification, turn-by-turn navigation or measured drive-test data.

Each route's first and last coordinates define its labeled Start and End points. The map line, endpoint markers and displayed coordinate text use the same route coordinates. View route fits the complete path in the visible map. Remove route edits the draft and takes effect after Save task; Delete task requires confirmation and removes its saved routes.

Task details and the lower Project Overview table accept comma-separated Drive speeds in km/h, such as 20, 40, 60. Each task can have up to 20 unique speed test cases from 0.1 to 200 km/h. Case IDs and default labels are generated and retained when an unchanged speed is reordered. Blank speeds mean unknown; no default is assumed. A legacy single speed becomes one case. Each case reuses the same task routes, rather than copying or rerouting geometry.

Project Overview places project context and sites above a full-width task-management table. New task creates a row; Edit task changes its ID, name and speeds; Save persists changes and Cancel restores saved values. Delete requires confirmation. A route-count button expands route names, fit status, distance, GPS positions and speed-case results. Edit routes on map carries the selected draft into the map editor. A pending draft can be resumed when returning to Overview. Failed saves retain edits. On phones the table scrolls horizontally, with task IDs kept visible.

Driving distance is the sum of two-dimensional geodesic route lengths. Each speed case has a separate estimated duration, calculated as kilometres divided by km/h and converted to seconds. Summaries show a duration range for multiple cases, and expanded details show individual case and per-route results. Estimates exclude connections between separate routes, stops, traffic delays and elevation effects. Unfitted paths are provisional. No route means no distance, duration or GPS endpoints; no speeds means duration remains unknown. These are planning estimates, not recorded driving time.

WGS84 Start and End use the first route's first point and last route's last point in list order. Per-route positions use that route's own geometry, including snapped endpoints after road fitting. These are planned positions rather than GPS measurements. Saved values are database-derived; current drafts are calculated in the browser before Save.

The root SQLite database stores individual task records in `drive_tasks`, routes in `drive_routes`, ordered path and guide points in `drive_route_points`, and test speeds in `drive_speed_cases`. SQL views `drive_task_details`, `drive_route_details`, `drive_speed_case_details` and `drive_route_case_details` expose distances, estimated durations and GPS data for querying. The API reads these views and returns accepted database records after saving. Writes replace a project's validated task list in one transaction, with foreign-key cascades for its child data. The transport remains a task array; the authoritative data is relational SQLite.

Existing `project_tasks` JSON lists migrate once into the typed tables and are retained in `project_tasks_legacy` as an archive that the application no longer updates or imports. IDs, route order, coordinates, guidance and extension attributes are preserved. A prior single-speed relational schema upgrades to speed-case rows while preserving its settings. Invalid or conflicting migration input rolls back rather than silently dropping records. A full SQLite backup should be retained before upgrade. SQLite window and math functions are required for the detail views.

A project supports at most 100 tasks, 50 routes per task, 2-5000 points per route and 10000 route points in total. Each route needs at least two distinct valid longitude/latitude positions. Import files and API requests are limited to 2 MB. Task loading and saving have a 10-second timeout. Failed loads disable editing until retry succeeds; failed saves retain the draft. Unsaved drafts are retained while switching projects during the current page session, and are lost on reload. Save or discard a task before selecting another task in the same project. SQLite stores task, route, point and case records separately from project site membership. Whole-list saves do not implement concurrent-user conflict resolution.

= Data, operation and limitations

- The supplied Toronto radio data is an ISED extract dated 2026-11-03; New York pole data was downloaded on 2026-11-07. Snapshot counts can change when the source data is rebuilt. OSM-derived roof, tree and street data is a dated extract, not a complete physical inventory.
- `make run` starts the local server on the first available port from 5179 through 5189. `PORT` selects an exact port. The default planning and drive-test databases are `var/regions.sqlite3` and `var/drive_tests.sqlite3`; project data and captures should be backed up.
- When a new default database is absent, startup copies the corresponding project-root database or, if absent, the older database under `ndt/var/`. Stop older viewer processes first to avoid split edits. Source files remain and existing destinations are not overwritten.
- The user-facing boundary is based on a fixed radio model, not site-specific surveyed equipment. The map can visualize indoor TELUS locations but does not calculate indoor coverage. The source does not support a per-site Bell/TELUS sharing assertion.
- The service binds to `127.0.0.1` by default. There is no implemented login or user-account system; if an operator changes the host binding, access to the service and project edits must be controlled outside the application.

