= Integrated drive tests and Parameter A/B

Open *Drive tests* from project navigation, *Open drive tests* in Project Overview, a Task row's *Drive tests* action, or a saved route's *Open drive tests* action. The workspace follows Region / Project / Task / Route / Test ID hierarchy and uses the viewer's green palette, typography and common basemap library. Save or discard Task changes before leaving. *Back to planning* restores the region, project and saved Task. One route is selected per execution; separate routes remain separate runs.

The workspace combines Test setup, Replay, KPI analytics, Investigate and Test report. Imported, synthetic demonstration and simulated Test IDs have distinct source labels. Import normalized UTF-8 CSV with timestamp, latitude, longitude and RAT headers, plus optional RF/service fields. One device per file, explicit timestamp offset, unique timestamps within each RAT and paired or missing GPS are required. Limits are 16 MiB and 100,000 observations. Validation precedes persistence and the original file SHA-256 is retained; original source bytes must be archived separately.

Replay synchronizes the trace cursor, UTC sample inspector, timeline and event list. Offline trace view works without tiles; NDT street map uses the same OpenFreeMap style as the main viewer. Missing GPS and temporal gaps break traces. Threshold excursions and observed cell changes describe the data; they do not establish handover outcomes. Planning runs label strongest-transmitter changes as predicted selections.

Analysis profiles persist separate LTE/NR thresholds for six measured metrics and planning received power, along with evidence criteria. Revision checks reject stale saves and keep the analyst's draft available. Readiness is assessed separately per selected RAT and metric. CDFs and whole-capture percentiles remain descriptive, sample-weighted statistics. Investigate searches and paginates observations, links findings to their source sample, and retains issue lifecycle and profile history. JSON includes complete current observations, source hashes, the effective profile and saved route comparison when present. Reports add a frozen route/experiment provenance sheet. CSV exports normalized fields.

== Task routes and measured traces

Test setup loads the served planning database's actual saved routes and speed cases. A launch freezes route/network context with hashes; a changed context rejects the launch until *Refresh planning inputs*. New road fits preserve complete `originalCoordinates`. Previously fitted records can lack their original geometry. Routes remain provisional or legacy-fit-unverified because fitting does not independently establish intended-road accuracy.

An imported capture can be associated with its selected Task route in forward or reverse direction. The association stores chainage, lateral error and matched, missing-GPS, off-route, ambiguous or wrong-direction status separately from raw observations. The current policy uses a 50 m tolerance and 10 s continuity cutoff. Associations are versioned and report continuous route coverage. This is a conservative single-pass geometric projection, not a road-network map matcher; repeated laps require separate captures or review.

== Virtual drives and power A/B

Choose one saved route, one or more saved speed cases, an assumed RAT label, LOS/NLOS, EIRP and a 0.1–10 s sampling interval. The initial model creates one isotropic planning transmitter per eligible Bell site at its lowest reported usable frequency. It uses fixed base/UE heights of 25 m and 1.5 m and the existing UMa mean path-loss formula within 10 m to 5 km. Missing or out-of-domain results remain missing. `predicted_rx_dbm` is planning received power, never measured RSRP or SS-RSRP. Static Virtual UE positions are not moved.

*Virtual drive* generates one run per speed. *Parameter A/B* sets a common baseline EIRP A across the frozen transmitter set and changes the selected transmitter's EIRP in B. Every pair shares route, speed, time grid, model and evaluation profile. Cases 20, 40 and 60 km/h produce A20/B20, A40/B40 and A60/B60. This static model gives speed a trajectory/time role; it does not model an independent speed-dependent RF effect. The legacy path has no antenna patterns or tilt. Directional applied-scenario runs are described below; interference, fading, mobility procedures, throughput and latency remain unavailable. New York reservation data lacks the required radio inputs, so import, association and replay remain available there while simulation is disabled.

A bounded local worker keeps queued, running, completed, failed, cancelled and interrupted experiment state in SQLite. Limits are 20 pending jobs, 100,000 observations per run and 200,000 total per experiment. Complete member Test IDs are published transactionally. Cancellation or partial failure cannot produce a completed experiment; completed members remain inspectable. A server interruption preserves history and requires a new submission for the interrupted job.

== Route comparison and reproducibility

KPI analytics can compare a baseline A and current B on common 20 m route bins. Both captures need the same geometry and Task; imported captures also need compatible direction/associations and adequate continuous coverage. The comparison requires at least 80 percent common coverage for every represented RAT. Power averages use linear power before conversion to dB. The saved result shows paired coverage, B minus A, a difference map and a keyboard-accessible position selector linked to Replay. Whole-capture distributions keep their separate sample-weighted meaning.

Simulated A/B quantifies the declared model's parameter effect. Imported A/B remains observational because device, traffic and capture conditions may differ. Cross-origin calibration is unavailable in the initial model. A comparison freezes both observation sets, hashes, parameters, association policies and evaluation profile; later planning, association or profile edits cannot change it. *Export experiment JSON* retains the completed speed/variant matrix and its comparisons.

On Windows 11, `make.ps1` provides the Makefile help, setup, run, test, test-ui and check targets. Use `-HostAddress` and `-Port` to set the server address, or the `HOST` and `PORT` environment variables. The default host is loopback and an omitted port enables the server fallback range. The PowerShell test-ui target checks for the locked npm packages and selected headless browser, installing missing dependencies with npm and Playwright. Browser installation needs network access and writes to the Playwright browser cache. The script requires uv, Node.js and npm.

= Radio planning workflow enhancements

The Network inspector shows site identity, reported RATs, licensed-record count, height, actual project memberships and coordinates before technical details. Selecting a member in Project Overview opens that site's inspector. All enabled licensed locations remain selectable even without a fan estimate. Site labels use provider and available ISED identifier at zoom 13 or closer; selected-site and named project-cluster labels remain visible at overview zooms. The label display has a 45-item bound; non-selected sites avoid nearby labels. The existing Bell planning group is separate from independently named inventory review clusters described below.

Site topology is a source-record hierarchy: site, reported bearing/non-directional/unknown group, then source records. All/LTE/NR filters support multi-RAT review. Bell aggregates do not identify individual cell-to-radio-to-antenna connections; those missing equipment links are stated explicitly. Search, topology, radio study, display controls and source notes use expandable sections to keep the default panel concise.

The independent radio study includes editable LTE and NR presets, reference-element, isotropic or uniform-panel patterns, carrier, bandwidth, conducted power, gain, feeder loss, azimuth and downtilt. Positive tilt points downward. It calculates peak EIRP, UMa path loss, directional attenuation and predicted received power at a distance/bearing probe. TR 38.901 v18.0.0 table 7.3-1 supplies the reference-element 65-degree widths and 30 dB caps; default peak gain is 8 dBi. Radio presets are study assumptions. Gain edits customize the element; isotropic mode requires 0 dBi and ignores tilt. Fixed BS 25 m and UE 1.5 m; 10 m to 5 km and 0.5 to 100 GHz. RAT and bandwidth do not change UMa arithmetic; optional pinned carrier checks are described below. The optional uniform-panel scalar array is described below. Interference, RSRP and throughput are unavailable. The editable probe does not change map fans or existing jobs. Applied qualified templates can be selected for new directional virtual drives.

Project studies now use the versioned SQLite scenario library. Region-only studies retain browser storage. Probe controls remain session inputs. Export JSON retains the template target, probe inputs, result, context, model reference and saved scenario history. Scenario application records a revision pointer that can be selected for a new directional virtual drive; it does not assign actual equipment or change existing jobs. Source-backed equipment imports and review are described below. Remaining physical equipment assignment and calibration work is recorded as planned in `docs/RADIO_PLANNING_ENHANCEMENT_PLAN.md`.

== Versioned project scenarios

Project studies can be named, saved, selected, copied and restored after reload. SQLite retains immutable template revisions, per-site template overrides, the regional source fingerprint, model version and creation time. Baseline and applied revision pointers remain fixed when a new draft revision is saved. A changed source fingerprint prevents applying an old revision until a new revision is saved. Apply saved revision records the immutable revision available for explicit selection in Drive tests. A newer saved draft does not change it. The probe uses editable inputs and map fans remain independent.

The browser distinguishes unsaved edits, draft revision, baseline and applied revision. Unsaved fields survive failed saves and project switches during the session. Concurrent or stale writes return a conflict without overwriting the current revision. Reload saved versions preserves a conflicting draft; Save as new scenario retains it under a new identity. Discard edits is explicit. A selected site can receive an assumed override, edited through Template target, without changing the default template. JSON export retains saved versions and labels unsaved edits. Legacy browser templates are recovered as unsaved drafts when the project has no server scenario.

The API is limited to same-origin localhost requests and validates complete template fields and known override site IDs. Transactions acquire an immediate write lock before comparing revisions. Limits are 20 scenarios per project, 50 retained revisions per scenario, 1000 site overrides and a 1 MB request. Scenario-library fingerprints identify an inventory version. A submitted experiment separately retains the complete contributing licensed records, regional metadata, optional original equipment file and applied scenario. Actual deployed-equipment assignment remains unavailable.

== Source-backed equipment inventory

Choose a project, open Equipment inventory imports and select a UTF-8 JSON file. Preview validates the file and shows its record counts, unmapped cells and source hash without changing saved data. Import previewed inventory appends an immutable revision. Reload preserves the selected file for another preview; conflicting or invalid imports do not overwrite saved equipment. Import history downloads the original source file, including any UTF-8 byte-order mark. The download link under Import format supplies the JSON schema.

Each imported entity has a stable ID, source ID and source record ID. Source records identify their title, URI, observation timestamp and operator/vendor/survey/assumption classification. Sites link to existing regional inventory IDs; sectors, cells, radios, antennas, ports and explicit connections form the equipment graph. Duplicate IDs, unresolved references and connections across sites are rejected. Unknown numeric fields use null and display Not reported; a reported zero remains zero. The imported claims are user-supplied evidence, not independent verification of physical deployment or operating status.

Selecting a site or New York reservation exposes Imported equipment topology with all/LTE/NR filters. Expand a cell to inspect its radio, TX port, connected antenna port, gain, downtilt and source records. Imported horizontal and vertical antenna cuts display their source samples in degrees and relative attenuation in dB. A missing connection remains unknown; licensed bearings never create equipment connections. These cuts remain inspection-only. An explicit cell/port path can seed the source-linked reference-element assumptions described below; vendor cuts are not assigned to predictions. Imported equipment cells are not qualified by the optional template carrier checks. Directional route runs require qualified study assignments; this does not validate imported equipment cells.

The importer supports schemaVersion 1, up to 2 MB of original UTF-8, 20,000 total records, 10,000 records per collection and 50 retained revisions per project. Cuts require 3–721 strictly ordered samples covering −180° to 180° horizontally and −90° to 90° vertically, a 0 dB peak and matching horizontal endpoints. No operator configuration or vendor measurement is bundled as verified deployment data; the test fixture is explicitly synthetic.

== Carrier channel checks and power convention

Carrier channel checks are optional within Radio and antenna study. Enable Check a supported 3GPP carrier, choose an operating band and enter a downlink channel number. Carrier frequency becomes read-only and follows EARFCN or NR-ARFCN. The view checks the selected RAT, channel range/raster, supported channel bandwidth, fixed supported SCS and full-channel band edges. An actionable error appears beside the carrier controls and disables Save study, Export JSON and probe calculation until corrected. A successful check shows only band, duplex and SCS in the panel; the exported JSON retains the pinned edition, plan version, reference and full template. Existing generic studies retain editable frequency and explicitly display Generic / not checked.

The supported downlink subset is LTE B2/B4/B7 with 5, 10, 15 or 20 MHz bandwidth and 15 kHz SCS, plus NR n41/n77/n78 with 30 kHz SCS and the band's supported bandwidth list. The pinned references are TS 36.104 and TS 38.104 v18.5.0, Release 18. LTE conversion uses table 5.7.3-1 and the 100 kHz channel raster. NR uses the global frequency raster and the band's 30 kHz channel raster in tables 5.4.2.1-1 and 5.4.2.3-1; bandwidths follow table 5.3.5-1. Requiring the complete channel inside the band is a conservative implementation constraint. Other bands/numerologies, uplink, carrier aggregation, DSS, FR2, SSB placement, resource allocations, spectrum licensing and device conformance are outside these checks.

TX power means total average conducted power for one modeled carrier/port. Peak-direction EIRP adds peak antenna gain and subtracts feeder loss. There is no resource or reference-signal power allocation, so the result remains received planning power rather than RSRP or SS-RSRP. Qualification does not change the fixed-height UMa formula or map fans. Directional virtual drives require a qualified applied scenario; generic legacy probes and isotropic route runs remain available and labeled separately.

== Applied scenarios in virtual drives

Apply a project scenario in Radio and antenna study, then select it in the Task's Drive tests workspace. The selector names its applied revision. The status shows RAT, assumed transmitter count and the selected transmitter's baseline EIRP. A newer saved draft is reported without silently applying it. Unsupported carrier assignments, changed regional sources or unavailable scenarios prevent launch with a specific issue. Legacy isotropic planning remains an explicit separate choice. Scenario mode hides the unused global A-power and environment controls; each frozen template supplies those values.

One assumed carrier is assigned to each eligible Toronto licensed planning site using the default template or its site override. A run uses only the selected RAT. Each template supplies frequency, LOS/NLOS, isotropic or reference-element pattern, azimuth, downtilt and peak EIRP from conducted power plus gain minus loss. Positive tilt points downward; isotropic orientation is ignored. Parameter A/B starts A from the applied template powers and changes only the selected transmitter's EIRP in B. A zero-change pair has identical finite predictions. For Parameter A/B, the library baseline pointer is retained separately; saved-scenario comparison below uses that pointer for A.

Submission atomically freezes the selected route/speeds, actual contributing licensed source records, regional metadata, saved membership, optional original equipment JSON including its UTF-8 byte-order mark, equipment revision/hash, complete applied scenario and pinned model/reference identities. Later edits cannot change queued or completed calculations. Old snapshots retain the legacy model; unknown model identities fail explicitly. Carrier validation happens on submission; a later live band-plan edit cannot reinterpret a queued job. Imported cell/port values can seed source-linked study assumptions; templates govern predictions and vendor pattern cuts remain source evidence only.

The result summary names its frozen scenario and revision. Editing setup shows Setup differs from saved results. Progress distinguishes completed experiments from the number of finished member runs. History remains available after a Task switch without late responses replacing the new Task's workspace. Reports include frozen source identity, assigned templates and effective powers; experiment JSON retains the complete snapshot and comparisons. Predictions remain uncalibrated planning received power within 10 m to 5 km with fixed 25 m BS and 1.5 m UE. No terrain/clutter, interference, reference-signal allocation, verified deployment or service prediction is added. New York reservation radio simulation remains unavailable.

== Named inventory clusters and table review

Project Overview now contains Site inventory and clusters for Toronto's Bell/TELUS source locations. Region/project context remains visible beside the inventory heading. Search site ID, provider or address; filter reported LTE/NR, Bell planning membership or the selected named group; sort by identity, provider or reported record count. The table renders 25 matching sites per page and scrolls within its own region on a phone. Review opens the existing map inspector; returning to Overview retains the selected row and filters. Visible Add/Remove planning project buttons provide touch and keyboard alternatives to the existing right-click menu. Bell additions retain the current three-usable-bearing eligibility rule; TELUS locations are outside that planning membership scope.

Choose Manage inventory clusters to create, rename or delete a review group. Add/remove individual site assignments in the table, then Save inventory groups. These named groups may overlap and can include both Bell and TELUS sites. They are independent inventory review metadata: they do not alter the Bell planning member list, radio scenario or simulation assignment. The map labels saved groups with project, group name and count; Show selected cluster on map fits the geographic extent of its currently available members. This extent is not a radio coverage boundary. The selected group/site have label priority with separate vertical offsets; normal collision and 45-label limits still apply.

Groups save with a project revision and current regional source check. Concurrent edits fail without replacing the local draft. Reload saved groups retains that draft and its original base revision; Use saved groups explicitly replaces it with the accepted saved version. Drafts survive project switches during the session. A late save cannot erase a newer draft. Unavailable source IDs remain retained and reported until Remove unavailable assignments is chosen. Limits are 50 named groups, 1000 unique regional site IDs per group, 10000 assignments per project and a 1 MB request. The table and map integration are Toronto features; New York retains its reservation/equipment review workflows.

Equipment file selection also stages a new empty read before decoding. A failed replacement cannot re-preview the previous file. File/decode/history replies are tied to their original context, so a late failure cannot cancel a new project's load. Preview/loading notices do not rebuild the selected equipment topology unless the accepted inventory changes.

== Saved baseline and applied scenario comparison

Set a baseline revision and an applied revision in the Radio and antenna study library. In the Task's Drive tests workspace, select the saved radio scenario and choose Scenario baseline/applied A/B. Setup identifies A baseline and B applied revision numbers. The scenario revisions supply carrier, conducted power, gain/loss, reference-element, isotropic or uniform-panel pattern, azimuth, downtilt and LOS/NLOS independently. The single-transmitter power controls are hidden. The same current eligible licensed project sites, route, speed cases, sampling/time grid and evaluation profile are used for both members; this is a comparison of study templates on the current planning inventory, not historical physical-network reconstruction.

Both revisions must have the current regional source fingerprint and supported carrier qualification, with assignments for a common selected RAT. A missing, outdated or unqualified baseline gives a specific issue and prevents submission. RAT choices follow the intersection of the revisions. A newer saved draft does not replace applied B. Submission freezes both complete payloads, derived assignments, role/revision summary, sources and selected-RAT input changes in one read transaction. Later library edits cannot change a queued pair. The existing reference-element prediction model is retained; array pairs use a separate identity described below; unknown frozen experiment types fail instead of being interpreted as a different experiment.

Run scenario comparison creates A/B for each selected speed. Results identify their frozen revision pair and expose Changed template inputs in expandable details, showing the first 100 changes with full export coverage. Recorded-only carrier qualification and bandwidth changes are distinguished from values used by the mean-power model; bandwidth at fixed carrier power does not create a predicted RF improvement. Isotropic orientation changes are also recorded-only. Identical inputs produce zero-change pairs. Pair comparison retains the existing route-coverage requirements, so insufficient paired evidence cannot become a completed comparison.

The report identifies the member's actual revision, the original A/B role numbers, its frozen templates, selected-RAT changes and paired result. Printed changes keep their heading/table together and show the first 20 with full details in JSON. Report deltas follow the existing display precision; exports retain exact numeric values. Parameter A/B remains the separate mode for changing one transmitter's EIRP from an applied study. Both modes remain uncalibrated planning received power, without measured RSRP, interference, terrain/clutter or service predictions.

== Source-linked reference-element site studies

In Toronto, expand Imported equipment topology and a cell with an explicit radio-port/antenna-port connection. Use the named port path in study to create an unsaved override for that licensed site. The action opens Radio and antenna study and its site target. It copies reported RAT, frequency, bandwidth, sector azimuth, antenna gain and downtilt. Missing copied values remain blank and prevent saving until the planner enters an explicit study assumption. Radio total power is not automatically interpreted as carrier/port power. Modeled power, feeder loss and environment come from the project study assumptions; the pattern is the 3GPP reference element, not the vendor cuts.

Exact carrier checks reset after copying because a rounded reported frequency does not establish an operating band/channel. Review the supported band and exact channel before using the override in a directional route. The source card identifies project/site/cell and source revision. Compare reported inputs exposes reported versus modeled values, missing inputs, edit counts and original-file hash; routine review stays concise. A site outside current eligible Bell run membership is identified. Keep as assumptions removes the provenance link while retaining the editable numbers. Template-target choices use available site labels. A source action waiting for library loading cannot edit a newly selected project.

Save study retains an optional equipment revision, SHA-256, cell ID and link ID inside the site override. Source links cannot be assigned to a default template and must trace an explicit same-site connection within the selected project's imported file. One equipment import revision is permitted per scenario. Historical links can be retained in saved revisions; applying a linked revision, setting its baseline or launching a new run requires the current import/hash and regional source identity. After a changed import, re-link reviewed equipment or explicitly retain numbers as assumptions. Queued/completed jobs retain their original source bytes and link even if later imports change.

Source linkage records provenance rather than independently verifying deployment, radio band support or equality of every modeled value to the source. Changed source-reference metadata alone has no RF effect in scenario A/B. Vendor two-dimensional cuts remain review-only; array geometry remains an explicit separate assumption and multi-port power allocation/full physical-cell execution remain unavailable. Virtual drives still use one assumed carrier per eligible licensed Bell planning site, fixed heights and the existing fixed-height UMa domain. Candidate and TELUS sources can inform a single-link study but do not create new virtual-drive membership automatically. Verified field calibration and operational-network claims still require suitable operator/measurement data.

== Uniform-panel array studies

Choose Uniform panel array to reveal geometry and electrical steering. The default 4-by-4 half-wavelength panel is an editable study assumption, not an operator equipment preset. Rows are vertical and columns horizontal. Enter 1 to 64 whole-number rows/columns, 0.25 to 1 wavelength spacing in each direction, and electrical azimuth/downtilt offsets from -90 to 90 degrees. Positive electrical azimuth is clockwise from panel boresight; positive electrical downtilt points downward in the mechanically rotated panel frame. The existing azimuth/downtilt controls orient the panel. Element peak gain is labeled separately. Changing an equipment-linked study to an array clears copied antenna gain and requires an explicit element-gain assumption. Copying a source path back into a study resets assumed array geometry and selects the reference element.

The model uses a single panel and polarization, the TR 38.901 v18.0.0 reference-element shape, and uniform coherent weights with total beam power shared across elements. The EIRP upper bound adds element peak gain and 10 log10(rows times columns) once, then subtracts feeder loss. Probe/route calculations also subtract element scan attenuation and the normalized array-factor attenuation. The bound need not be attained when steering off element boresight. Spacing above half a wavelength may create grating lobes. Ideal null attenuation is capped numerically at 120 dB, an application convention rather than a 3GPP parameter. Slant distance must meet the far-field boundary 2D squared over wavelength using the diagonal aperture D. Unsupported route samples remain unavailable with an explanatory event; route power below -300 dBm is also unavailable, rather than clipped or treated as valid measured signal.

Array geometry is retained in saved defaults/site overrides, independent probe JSON, frozen directional runs and baseline/applied comparisons. Reports show readable geometry/steering. Reports and execution summaries format EIRP upper-bound powers to one decimal; JSON retains numeric precision and model assumptions. Existing isotropic/reference-element runs keep their original execution model identities. The new scalar array model is narrowband and far-field: it provides no mutual coupling, polarization mismatch, multi-port allocation, measured vendor reconstruction or MIMO/channel-matrix/service prediction. The geometry and beam power do not establish physical deployment or reference-signal allocation. Carrier qualification and current-source requirements still apply to route execution.

